hromium-review.googlesource.com/318384 Commit-Ready: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Brian Wilson &lt;brian.wilson@intel.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Dylan Reid &lt;dgreid@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="2" gerrit_number="318384" owner_email="joe.konno@linux.intel.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/overlays/portage-stable" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/portage-stable" ref="refs/changes/84/318384/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I80f8183bc20473e6c21fe03c0331a3c040624b5d" commit="4cf7527a62d74fb4895433e624786ce457efbc68" commit_message="Make generate_test_report point at autotest copy  BUG=b:25929635 TEST=`./generate_test_report` prints usage message  CQ-DEPEND=CL:316524  Change-Id: I80f8183bc20473e6c21fe03c0331a3c040624b5d Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/316457 Commit-Ready: Christopher Wiley &lt;wiley@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Christopher Wiley &lt;wiley@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Simran Basi &lt;sbasi@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="316457" owner_email="wiley@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/crosutils" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/crosutils" ref="refs/changes/57/316457/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I9b56605e4f760f162d8be96e71bc887cdb0c2ea1" commit="51f4addafd71384f5a44f2eeef1d787f42136d00" commit_message="Remove generate_test_report from cros-testutils  This script has been moved into the autotest code base.  BUG=b:25929635 TEST=None  CQ-DEPEND=CL:316457  Change-Id: I9b56605e4f760f162d8be96e71bc887cdb0c2ea1 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/316577 Commit-Ready: Christopher Wiley &lt;wiley@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Christopher Wiley &lt;wiley@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Simran Basi &lt;sbasi@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="316577" owner_email="wiley@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/crostestutils" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/crostestutils" ref="refs/changes/77/316577/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I082de93441d3edf7e1337cd4754e8e5acb521c42" commit="5bd9b8a0606b6cb8fce6b1b73aa94ff2f6b0c5e1" commit_message="mkbp: Ensure mkbp_keyboard_havekey() always returns correct results  The MKBP protocol works by requesting keyboard *states* (not keys) from the EC via host command. The EC can buffer a certain number of states and enqueues a new state in its FIFO whenever any key changed from the previous one. Depthcharge extracts pressed keys by comparing the last two states and extracting differences (potentially filtering out some stuff we don't care about).  The problem is that depthcharge's mkbp_keyboard_havekey() function only requests up to one new keyboard state per invocation. If no actual keys result from analyzing that state, it assumes that there are no more keys buffered. This assumption can fail in multiple ways, such as when several individual presses are enqueued in the EC FIFO: pressing 'A' and then 'B' will queue up four states (&lt;only A pressed&gt;, &lt;nothing pressed&gt;, &lt;only B pressed&gt;, &lt;nothing pressed&gt;). The first invocation of mkbp_keyboard_havekey() after the mkbp_keyboard_getchar() that returns 'A' will thus return 0, because the state retrieved by it did not reveal any new actual key presses.  This patch fixes the issue by always requesting as many keyboard states as it takes to either recognize an actual key press or completely exhaust the EC's FIFO.  BRANCH=none? BUG=chromium:570814 TEST=Tested together with next patch.  Change-Id: I082de93441d3edf7e1337cd4754e8e5acb521c42 Signed-off-by: Julius Werner &lt;jwerner@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319183 Reviewed-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319183" owner_email="jwerner@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" ref="refs/changes/83/319183/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ib7e097cc4a8d5dac87356e4744d51cc249df2adb" commit="b1c504b01651c94c65f0cce5058ac903a47a4cfb" commit_message="mkbp: Flush EC keyboard state FIFO during initialization  On MKBP devices the EC can buffer a certain number of key states until the AP is ready to read them. Since the EC usually doesn't (directly) notice a warm reboot, this can capture keys a user was typing while the machine crashed and then deliver them to depthcharge. Since this can result in unexpected and potentially disastrous effects ([Space, Enter] to leave developer mode and clear the TPM), we should flush out the EC's FIFO before we start interpreting keys.  Note: This patch means that people can no longer just blindly hit Ctrl+D after hitting the power button and have it work most of the time... they'll have to pay attention and wait until the developer screen is shown for it to work. I don't know if anyone will actually notice or be annoyed by this, and the benefits probably still outweigh it, but I just wanted to point it out.  BRANCH=none? BUG=chromium:570814 TEST=Pressed a bunch of keys during coreboot, noticed how they were printed by the new &quot;ignoring key code&quot; message and no longer by vboot.  Change-Id: Ib7e097cc4a8d5dac87356e4744d51cc249df2adb Signed-off-by: Julius Werner &lt;jwerner@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319184 Reviewed-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319184" owner_email="jwerner@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" ref="refs/changes/84/319184/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I196ac339e8e1602a642d412ec66717a67a2f42c5" commit="3b83267dc4f0e6c2456a5dda5c58f9767aba7a05" commit_message="CHROMIUM: clk: rockchip: Add 621 MHz to better approx 88.75 HDMI  If we divide 621 by 7 we get 88.714... MHz, which is slightly closedr to 88.75 than we had before.  Also this frequency is very high Fvco so presumably the low jitter will also help us have a better chance of working with people that expect 88.75.  If displays are still failing with this then we may have to give up on 88.75 since we can't make that exact rate on rk3288 without an Fvco of 8.52 GHz which is above even the extended 5 GHz max.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:48663 TEST=Use HDMI with 88.75 MHz.  It works OK.  Change-Id: I196ac339e8e1602a642d412ec66717a67a2f42c5 Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319254 Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319254" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/54/319254/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I65c493821c636b8eab0302cf08890a8f44b39a38" commit="2314482f300132fca156453271a76450b90fc3cc" commit_message="CHROMIUM: drm: rockchip/dw_hdmi: Make 88.75 approx a little better  We added a new PLL rate of 621 MHz, which allows us to get a little closer to 88.75.  Let's use this and see if it makes folks wanting 88.75 work better.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:48663 TEST=Use HDMI with 88.75 MHz.  It works OK.  Change-Id: I65c493821c636b8eab0302cf08890a8f44b39a38 Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319255 Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319255" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/55/319255/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I81b0f42a85716a413dbdf77733efde11e60adc04" commit="6a4f7b31732c95dba002cf4ac75d7d74ab55e40d" commit_message="[autotest] Fix unittest flake in suite_scheduler/driver_unittest.py  mox does not work well with multiprocessing. By forcing the thread pool size to be 1, the method check flake was able to be resolved.  BUG=chromium:570891 TEST=local run for i in `seq 1 10000`; do python site_utils/suite_scheduler/driver_unittest.py || { exit 1; } done | tee 1.log  Change-Id: I81b0f42a85716a413dbdf77733efde11e60adc04 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/319222 Commit-Ready: Dan Shi &lt;dshi@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Dan Shi &lt;dshi@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mungyung Ryu &lt;mkryu@google.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="319222" owner_email="dshi@google.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/22/319222/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Iaf295e711b5e855e80b7a084765d19b4f5118fb9" commit="5b809258384af73745435cbab3acc5a454723475" commit_message="lint: flag optparse as deprecated  We want people to use chromite.lib.commandline or at least argparse. Mark optparse as deprecated so people won't write new code with it.  BUG=None TEST=`cros lint` doesn't show any new warnings (after adding markers)  Change-Id: Iaf295e711b5e855e80b7a084765d19b4f5118